WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.010
We have new job numbers and they're pretty
ugly, quite frankly.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:09.910
So before the start of the Corona virus pandemic,
the U.S. had about one hundred and fifty eight

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.580
million people working.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:19.730
Now, you probably have heard Republicans bragging
about how more people were working before

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:22.619
the Corona virus pandemic than ever before.

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:23.619
And that's true.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:27.500
But also, more people were not working before
the coronavirus pandemic than ever before.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.890
This is a very popular political football
that I want to remind you of before we move

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.870
on to the data, which is the U.S. population
grows.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:39.660
So if you have roughly the same portion of
the population working and not working at

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.969
almost any point in time, you can accurately
say we have more people than ever working

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:49.560
or you can accurately say we have more people
than ever not working.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.780
Both are often true because the population
keeps getting bigger when it's a Democratic

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:54.780
president.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.800
The Republicans focus on the number of people
not working.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.450
When it's a Republican president, they focus
on the number of people working.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.810
It's a meaningless statistic.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:06.130
Absent population growth sort of rates.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.740
And that is where that talking point comes
from.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.229
But needless to say, one hundred and fifty
eight million people were working prior to

00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:15.770
the start of the Corona virus pandemic.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:21.270
We now have a total of at least twenty two
point three million people who have lost their

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.270
jobs.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:27.869
Today we learned that another five and a quarter
million people filed for unemployment benefits

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:28.869
last week.

00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:30.450
We get the data on a one week delay.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.439
So today we learned about the numbers from
last week.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:40.009
This brings the total to about 21 million
people who have filed for unemployment benefits

00:01:40.009 --> 00:01:42.880
since the start of the Corona virus pandemic.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:50.000
This is 13 percent of the working population
that have lost their jobs.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.649
And the the real number is even higher.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:58.240
And what I mean by that is there are systems
at the state level for filing for unemployment

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.569
benefits that are over the max.

00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:01.679
They are stretched.

00:02:01.679 --> 00:02:04.809
They are overwhelmed phone systems where it's
just busy.

00:02:04.809 --> 00:02:07.279
They can't even put you in the hold queue.

00:02:07.279 --> 00:02:11.069
Web systems that are just crashing because
of the number of people trying to file benefits.

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:13.760
So this is the number of people that have
been able to file benefits.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.370
You've got all the people who have tried but
failed.

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:22.659
You also have people working in under the
table jobs who do not qualify for employment.

00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:25.909
Unemployment benefits traditionally, even
if exceptions have been made so that they

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:27.750
do in this particular case.

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:31.510
Many of them don't know it and they've simply
lost their jobs and not even filed.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.359
So the real rate is much higher.

00:02:33.359 --> 00:02:38.780
Now we have a situation where the unemployment
rate, as we typically see it.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.830
This is called the U-3 rate.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.099
It's going to be almost useless very soon.

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:49.689
And I'll explain why the U3 is going to go
up, because tens of millions of people have

00:02:49.689 --> 00:02:56.349
lost their jobs, but it's likely to go up
only a fraction of the sort of real unemployment

00:02:56.349 --> 00:02:57.349
rate.

00:02:57.349 --> 00:03:02.290
For this reason and it's counterintuitive,
you might think if 13 percent of the workforce

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:07.530
lost its job, how is it that the unemployment
rate might only go from like four to six or

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:08.530
four to seven percent?

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:09.930
It doesn't make sense.

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:15.239
The way our system works, the U3, which is
that rate that is publicized, though, it's

00:03:15.239 --> 00:03:17.159
3.8, it's 4.2.

00:03:17.159 --> 00:03:22.530
When you aren't looking for work, you are
no longer considered unemployed from the purposes

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.120
of the unemployment rate.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.829
This has always been the case.

00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:30.599
And you know, during Obama, as usual, as the
unemployment rate went down, you had Republicans

00:03:30.599 --> 00:03:32.950
say the system is bogus.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:36.579
If you stop looking for work, you're no longer
considered unemployed.

00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:37.980
That's been the case for decades.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.580
So, again, it's another distraction technique
that the right loves to use.

00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:47.959
But in this particular very special situation,
there are going to be millions of people who

00:03:47.959 --> 00:03:51.650
have just lost their jobs, who are not looking
for work, because normally when you file for

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:55.939
benefits, you have a questionnaire to fill
out or you might be doing it online, you might

00:03:55.939 --> 00:04:00.430
be answering it on the phone and you are asked,
are you looking for work?

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.230
Have you interviewed anywhere for work?

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:03.609
Give us a list of where you have it.

00:04:03.609 --> 00:04:05.489
You need to do that in order to get benefits.

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:10.279
If you don't say that you're doing that, you
don't get the benefits because so many of

00:04:10.279 --> 00:04:14.049
the current job losses are pandemic related
job losses.

00:04:14.049 --> 00:04:16.010
That is not required.

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:18.350
You are not required to be looking for work.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.910
You're only required to be keeping in touch
with your employer.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:27.941
So this could create a very confusing situation
where despite 22 million people, 21 million

00:04:27.941 --> 00:04:32.840
people losing their jobs, we may only see
a couple point uptick in the official unemployment

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:36.800
rate because that's the way the unemployment
rate is calculated.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.300
Republicans would complain about this during
Obama's term.

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.020
They'd say the economy is much worse than
the rate tells you because all these people

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:50.099
are so sick of looking for work in this terrible
Obama economy, which wasn't true, that they've

00:04:50.099 --> 00:04:51.610
stopped looking.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:56.410
And so really the the U-3 is inaccurately
low.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:57.410
That's true.

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:58.450
But that's always the case here.

00:04:58.450 --> 00:04:59.450
This.

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:03.840
Really matters because you are not required
to be looking for work to receive unemployment

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:04.949
benefits.

00:05:04.949 --> 00:05:09.580
And then there's the issue of the real number
is way higher overwhelmed unemployment systems.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:13.389
As I mentioned, people trying to file claims
but unable to.

00:05:13.389 --> 00:05:17.450
People who have lost jobs where they traditionally
don't get unemployment benefits.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:22.979
So the true number, sadly, is way higher than
21 million people now.

00:05:22.979 --> 00:05:28.539
In real terms, how many of these 21 million
jobs are going to reappear as the pandemic

00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:30.340
starts to fade?

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.090
The answer is we don't know.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:35.160
Number one, when we talk about the pandemic
fading, there's not going to be one day where

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:36.160
we flip a switch.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.160
It's gonna be a process.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:41.540
It's a process that could take up to 18 months
in some ways, shapes or forms.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:44.970
One report said four restaurants, for example.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.250
July is going to be the limit for a lot of
restaurants.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:52.580
At which point many restaurant owners will
just start walking away from their leases

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.639
and not planning to reopen.

00:05:54.639 --> 00:06:02.050
Really, really sad because yesterday was the
record death rate, the number of deaths, rather,

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:05.229
in both the United States and globaly.

00:06:05.229 --> 00:06:09.770
So we are not exactly at a time where we should
be planning to reopen, despite Donald Trump

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:14.960
announcing yesterday that today he would give
a list of states that he wants to see reopen

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:16.000
right away.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.530
One other data point, just out of curiosity,
we did a survey of our own audience and we

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:25.550
asked, has your income decreased due to Corona
virus?

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.870
Almost two thirds of our audience says they
are.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:31.030
Their income is the same or higher during
the virus.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:35.449
Eleven percent saw their income decrease anywhere
from 10 to 30 percent.

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:38.940
Nine percent said their income is down 40
to 70 percent.

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.000
Three percent said their income is down 80
to 90 percent.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.180
But 14 percent of our viewers and listeners
say their income dropped to zero.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:52.510
Notice that 14 percent of our audience has
lost all their income.

00:06:52.510 --> 00:06:58.280
It looks like 13 percent of the workforce
nationally has gone on unemployment benefits.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.039
So very similar numbers.

00:07:00.039 --> 00:07:01.580
Numbers tracking very similarly there.

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:05.169
So we've got a big uphill battle to get back
to where we were.

00:07:05.169 --> 00:07:10.820
Industries may change discussions of nationalizing
certain supply chains are already starting,

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:14.720
whether it's masks or medical equipment or
whatever, because of the virus.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:20.379
Anybody pretending to know what the economic
landscape is going to look like in 18 months

00:07:20.379 --> 00:07:21.650
is simply guessing.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:24.910
And we need to understand that we need to
sit with the uncertainty because we really

00:07:24.910 --> 00:08:31.590
don't have another choice.

